  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 119 AM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nAn upper level ridge will amplify over the plains over the next\nseveral days, leading to very hot weather. For today, areas that\nreceived heavy rain will be more humid. A heat advisory remains\nin effect for the south where the heat and moisture will lead \nto heat indices in excess of 105. An isolated shower or t-storm \ncan't be ruled out across the far southwest corner of Kansas \nwith afternoon heating; but the best chance will be south of \nthe state line with where a weak frontal boundary will reside.\n\nThe various ensemble means continue to indicate high\ntemperatures between 105 and 111 for Sunday through Tuesday\ngiven the strong upper level ridge. There is some indication \nthat the intense heat could last through Wednesday, especially \nfrom Dodge City southward. There is general agreement among the\nensemble means that a front will progress into central Kansas \nby Tuesday or Wednesday. There is a chance that the front could \nmake it farther south. However, even if a frontal passage \noccurs, it is often oppressive on the cooler but moist side of \nfronts this time of year where very high dewpoints typically \nreside. Therefore, north of the front it will be hot and humid \nwhile south of the front it will be very hot and dry. Either way\nit will feel very hot. Isolated t-storms can't be ruled out \nwherever the front stalls out given the moisture and frontal \nconvergence; but with a very weak shear profile expected, the \nstorms will likely be sub-severe, or at best marginally severe \nwith high winds. The grand ensemble shows chances of .5\" or \nmore of rain between 0 and 5% next week.",
